;; WRITTEN BY THE SETTINGS FORM, once you use it. The comments this file carries
;; now are this template's; a write from the settings panel rewrites the file whole
;; and does not keep them (EDN has no way to), leaving the previous version beside
;; it as config.edn.bak. Editing by hand is still fine -- the form reads this file
;; back -- and the next write from the form will reorder it again.
;;
;; THE ONE CONFIG FILE. Its top level is exactly six sections:
;;
;;   :default    the three knobs a session starts from (or a provider DESCRIBED
;;               inline -- the escape hatch, below)
;;   :providers  the vendor catalog: which vendors this harness can reach, and
;;               which models each one serves
;;   :ui         the interface's own settings -- today just :language, the language
;;               this harness speaks (see the note below)
;;   :security   extra paths this home calls sensitive, ON TOP OF the built-in list: a file
;;               tool aimed at one of them parks for your approval -- reads park for this
;;               reason only (they are not limited by the project fence), writes park here
;;               as well as by the fence
;;   :session    how a session runs: editing / compaction / llm / approval / skills /
;;               instructions / subagents, plus :groups -- per-model overrides of those
;;               blocks (what harness.edn used to hold, keys unchanged)
;;   :mcp        the outside programs whose tools join this session: {:servers {..}}
;;
;; SIX SECTIONS, ONE FILE, because they are all facts about this ONE HOME -- which vendors
;; this machine can reach, which one it starts on, which language it speaks, which paths it
;; will not let a tool read without asking, how a session edits and compacts, and which
;; outside programs it hands tools to. harness.edn and mcp.edn used to be two more files
;; (.scratch/config-merge); a home that still has one is migrated at boot, and the file is
;; moved beside it as `<name>.bak`.
;; vendors this machine can reach, which one it starts on, and which language it
;; speaks -- and a person changing any of them should not have to open three files.
;; (What used to be providers.edn is the :providers section. A home that still has
;; that file is refused by name, with a sentence saying to move its entries here and
;; delete it.)
;;
;; THE TOP LEVEL IS CLOSED: a key that is not one of those sections is a named
;; failure. The case that matters is the shape this file used to have -- :provider /
;; :model / :reasoning-effort sitting at the top, with no sections at all. There is
;; no migration and no second shape read: the failure says to put them under :default.

{:default {:provider :openrouter :model "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5" :reasoning-effort "high"}

 :ui {:language :en}                        ; English (en) or Chinese (zh)
 :security {:sensitive-paths ["~/.ssh/" "~/.aws/" "~/.config/gh/hosts.yml"]}  ; added on top of the built-in list; [] adds none

 :session
 { :editing
  {;; Which file-editing implementation this session is served. One per session,
   ;; never both: the two hand the model different workspaces (a per-line anchor
   ;; versus an exact old_string), and a model that can see both reaches for
   ;; whichever it recognises first.
   ;;
   ;;   :hashline     read returns `anchor│content` rows; replace / insert address
   ;;                 those anchors; the answer to an edit carries the changed
   ;;                 region WITH its current anchors, so the next edit needs no
   ;;                 re-read. THE DEFAULT.
   ;;   :str-replace  the original `edit`: an exact old_string, refused when it is
   ;;                 absent or not unique.
   ;;
   ;; Switching is one line and loses nothing -- both are first-class and both
   ;; have complete test coverage.
   :mode :hashline

   ;; The `grep` tool (ripgrep, every hit back as an anchored row). false
   ;; takes the tool out of the session's toolset; nothing replaces it, and `bash`
   ;; is there if you need a search.
   :grep true

   ;; Require `path` on replace / insert, and check it against the anchor's owner.
   ;; Off by default: an anchor already names exactly one file in one session, so
   ;; the path is redundant unless a caller wants it said out loud.
   :require-path false

   ;; Refuse the slips an edit's payload can carry (a pasted row, an embedded
   ;; newline, a whole JSON array in one element) instead of fixing them. false --
   ;; the default -- fixes them and SAYS what it fixed.
   :strict-input false

   ;; How many untouched lines an edit's answer shows either side of the change
   ;; (0-10).
   :diff-context-lines 1}

 ;; Context compaction. `:threshold-ratio` is the pressure (a fraction of the window) at
 ;; which a run compacts BEFORE its request goes out; `:retain-ratio` is the newest slice
 ;; kept VERBATIM. Retain must stay STRICTLY below threshold. `:overflow-retries` bounds the
 ;; retry after a vendor has refused a request for LENGTH (`0` disables it): the recovery
 ;; compacts more aggressively, and retries only when the model view really got shorter.
 ;; BEFORE a summary, an oversized TOOL RESULT is elided for free (`harness.edge.prune`): head
 ;; and tail kept, no model call, and the pressure is measured again -- under the threshold, the
 ;; summary is skipped. Those cut points are constants in that namespace, not knobs here.
 :compaction
 {:threshold-ratio  0.7
  :retain-ratio     0.16
  :overflow-retries 1}

 ;; THE IDLE GUARD. A model call that says nothing for `:idle-timeout-ms` (default 30 s) is
 ;; treated as a dead connection: the response body is closed, the call is abandoned, and --
 ;; when its ANSWER had not yet begun -- tried again, up to `:idle-timeout-retries` times.
 ;; THINKING COUNTS AS NOT YET ANSWERING (2026-10-02): an attempt that had only streamed
 ;; reasoning is tried again exactly like one that had said nothing, while text or a tool call
 ;; on the wire ends the run instead.
 ;; THE DEADLINE IS GENEROUS ON PURPOSE (it was 500 ms until 2026-09-29): half a second
 ;; cut off vendors that were merely slow to their first byte, and the answer that arrived
 ;; afterwards poisoned the attempt that followed. `0` for either knob turns it off (no
 ;; deadline / no retry), which is what a session talking to a vendor that never answers -- or
 ;; one that thinks for minutes before its first token -- wants. The retries are PER CALL and
 ;; reset on every call, so a long turn is not spending one budget across its rounds. See
 ;; .scratch/llm-idle-timeout/spec.md; the two knobs are read by harness.edge.llm-timeout.
 ;;
 ;; A timeout is shown in the conversation as a custom frame and is NEVER written to the
 ;; record: it is a fact about a call in flight, not part of the conversation.
 :llm
 {:idle-timeout-ms      30000
  :idle-timeout-retries 3}

  ;; The project fence. When a session is bound to a project directory, a file
  ;; tool whose path resolves outside the project directory AND the configuration
  ;; home parks for human approval before it runs.
  :approval
  {;; Extra paths to treat as inside the fence. Relative paths resolve against
   ;; the project root.
   :allow []

   ;; true takes the project directory ITSELF out of the allowed set: every file
   ;; tool parks, even for files in the project. The configuration home is never
   ;; tightened.
   :strict false}

  ;; THE TOOLS a session is served, and how they behave. One knob today.
  :tools
  {;; The longest a `bash` call may wait, in milliseconds -- a HARD CEILING and not a
   ;; default: a call that names no `timeout` waits two minutes, while a call asking for
   ;; more than this is REFUSED rather than quietly shortened, and the tool's own
   ;; description says so. 600000 (ten minutes) is the harness's own ceiling, and this
   ;; knob can only LOWER it -- a larger number is refused by name, since a limit a
   ;; configuration can raise is not a limit. Read fresh on every call, like everything
   ;; else in this section, so an edit takes effect without a restart.
   :bash-max-timeout-ms 600000}

  ;; GROUPS -- THE PER-MODEL OVERRIDES. The blocks above are the DEFAULT group, and
  ;; they serve EVERY model. Each entry of :groups names the models it serves and lays
  ;; its own blocks over the default group's, KEY BY KEY: this one says only that
  ;; sonnet-4.5 edits with str-replace and never gives up on a silent vendor -- the
  ;; default group's :compaction, :approval and :skills still stand. A session is served
  ;; by the groups whose :models match the model it resolved to (a later matching group
  ;; wins a key an earlier also named), and by the default group underneath them all.
  ;;
  ;; A group is {:name .. :models [..] ..any of the seven blocks above..}. :name is a
  ;; non-empty, unique string; :models is a non-empty list of {:provider :x :model ".."},
  ;; where :provider may be left out to say 'this id, from whoever serves it'. :subagents
  ;; is NOT a group key -- a subagent is a fact about this home, not about a model -- so
  ;; it keeps its own settings page; a group that names it fails by name.
  :groups
  [{:name   "str-replace-editing"
    :models [{:provider :openrouter :model "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5"}]
    :editing {:mode :str-replace}
    :llm     {:idle-timeout-ms 0}}]}

 :mcp  {:servers {}}


 :providers
 {:openrouter
  {:protocol :openai-completions
   :base-url "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
   :model    "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5"                 ; the DEFAULT model id
   :models   {"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5" {:input #{:text :image} :output #{:text}
                                             :context-window 1000000
                                             :max-output-tokens 64000}
              "anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5"  {:input #{:text :image} :output #{:text}
                                             :context-window 200000
                                             :max-output-tokens 64000}
              "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro"    {:input #{:text}       :output #{:text}
                                             :context-window 1048576
                                             :max-output-tokens 393216}
              "openai/gpt-4o-mini"          {:input #{:text :image} :output #{:text}
                                             :context-window 128000
                                             :max-output-tokens 16384}}}

  :local
  {:protocol :openai-completions
   :base-url "http://localhost:11434/v1"   ; ollama, or any OpenAI-compatible
   :model    "qwen3"                       ; endpoint you run yourself
   :models   {"qwen3"    {:input #{:text}       :output #{:text}
                          :context-window 40960}
              ;; a tag like :30b carries 256K -- the built-in entry records the
              ;; `latest` tag's window, so a different tag is a line like this one
              "qwen3-vl" {:input #{:text :image} :output #{:text}
                          :context-window 262144}}}}}

;; :default -- THE THREE KNOBS, and only three. Anything left out falls through:
;; to the provider's own default model, or to no reasoning effort at all, so
;; naming only a provider is the simplest working case.
;;
;;   :provider          a vendor from :providers, or from the built-in table
;;   :model             a model id THAT vendor declares (optional; leaving it out
;;                      uses the vendor's default)
;;   :reasoning-effort  forwarded to providers that understand it
;;
;; A model's context window and maximum output tokens are NOT here: those describe
;; what a model IS, so they live on that model's entry in :providers, and writing
;; one here is a named failure rather than a value somebody quietly drops.
;;
;; THE INLINE FORM (the escape hatch): describe a provider instead of naming one,
;; for trying an endpoint without registering it. Requires :protocol, :base-url
;; and :model; :input/:output are optional, and declaring nothing means the entry
;; promises nothing (nothing then checks its input). The two counts are optional
;; here too, and like everywhere else they are REPORTED rather than enforced --
;; :max-output-tokens is not sent to the vendor and :context-window blocks no run.
;;
;;   {:default {:protocol :openai-completions
;;              :base-url "https://some-endpoint/v1"
;;              :model    "some-model"
;;              :input    #{:text :image}
;;              :output   #{:text}
;;              :context-window 128000
;;              :max-output-tokens 4096}}

;; :providers -- A PROVIDER IS A VENDOR: one protocol, one endpoint, listing the
;; models reachable there.
;;
;; :model is the id served unless a tier picks another one, and it must be a key of
;; :models. :input / :output are REQUIRED of every model, and the vocabulary is
;; what this harness can actually move:
;;
;;   :input   :text and/or :image    (a model declaring :audio is claiming a
;;                                    capability the wire cannot deliver)
;;   :output  :text only             (harness reads text, reasoning and tool
;;                                    calls out of a response, and nothing else)
;;
;; :context-window / :max-output-tokens are OPTIONAL counts a model may state --
;; how much context it holds and how much it may give back, both positive
;; integers. They are REPORTED, NOT ENFORCED, and that is worth knowing before
;; writing one:
;;
;;   * nothing here counts tokens, so :context-window never blocks a run -- an
;;     estimate used as a rule is a guess wearing a rule's clothes;
;;   * :max-output-tokens is NOT sent as max_tokens -- the vendor's own default
;;     decides, and this harness does not second-guess it.
;;
;; They are for whoever is CHOOSING a model: GET /api/model, the provider/init and
;; provider/changed lines in the session log. Omit a count you have not verified --
;; an absent field means 'this catalog does not know', which is honest, while a
;; plausible guess would be the same class of lie as an unverified model id.
;; Declaring both? :max-output-tokens must not exceed :context-window.
;;
;; THIS SECTION IS OPTIONAL. A :default naming a provider from the built-in table
;; (harness.cap.providers) needs no entry at all, and the inline form above needs
;; neither. What you write here is merged OVER the built-in table: same provider
;; field by field, same provider's :models model by model -- and an individual
;; MODEL merges field by field too, so correcting one count does not mean restating
;; that model's modalities. Your entries win. That is also what makes a one-line
;; patch legitimate:
;;
;;   {:providers {:openrouter {:base-url "https://my-proxy/v1"}}}
;;
;; Every entry is validated whenever the file is read, so a malformed one fails on
;; the next run BY NAME rather than quietly serving something else.
;;
;; A LOCAL ENDPOINT NEEDS NO KEY, but harness.cap.providers still resolves
;; HARNESS_API_KEY (from .env, then the environment) and attaches it -- the
;; protocol module decides whether to use it. Nothing to configure for localhost.
;;
;; reasoning-effort is NOT a provider field. It is one of the three knobs, so it
;; belongs in :default (or in a POST /api/model call -- what the picker presses).
;;
;; :ui -- THE INTERFACE'S OWN SETTINGS. Today one key:
;;
;;   :language  :en or :zh -- the language this harness speaks, to the reader and to
;;              the model. Read fresh on every run, so an edit takes effect without
;;              a restart. ABSENT is the everyday case and not a failure: when this
;;              names no language, the OS's own language is asked, then the
;;              terminal's, and English is the end of that chain (see
;;              harness.infra.language). A KEY OR A VALUE THIS SECTION DOES NOT
;;              KNOW IS a named failure -- a typo here must not sit there doing
;;              nothing.
;;
;; :security -- PATHS THIS HOME CALLS SENSITIVE, ON TOP OF THE BUILT-IN LIST, and the only
;; section that is not about a model or an interface at all. A file tool aimed at one of
;; them PARKS for a human first -- INCLUDING when the path is inside the project, which is
;; the case a project bound at your home directory makes. It is a list of PATHS, and the
;; criterion is OVERLAP: a path inside one of them, or a directory that CONTAINS one (a
;; grep over a directory reads what is under it).
;;
;; READS AND WRITES DIFFER HERE. read, grep and glob are held ONLY by this list: they are
;; NOT limited by the project fence, so a read outside the bound project runs. write, edit,
;; replace and insert are held by this list AND by the fence (a write outside the free
;; paths parks on the fence as before).
;;
;; THE LIST IS ALWAYS IN FORCE, BUILT-IN FIRST: harness.cap.providers/default-sensitive-paths
;; (the cloud CLIs, key material and keychains, container configuration, and the credential
;; files of the package managers and of git) can NEVER be switched off. What this section
;; writes is the CUSTOM half, ADDED on top. `[]` is a decision -- 'add none of my own' --
;; and the built-in list is still there; it is not 'guard nothing'.
;;
;; THIS SECTION IS ABSENT BY DEFAULT: no `:security` means no custom entries, and the
;; built-in list stands alone.
;;
;; TO SEE WHAT IS IN FORCE ask GET /api/security (it answers `builtin`, `custom`, and the
;; union under `sensitive-paths`), or open the settings panel -- which writes the custom
;; half for you and draws the built-in half as not deletable, so the list can be kept up to
;; date without editing this file by hand.
;;
;; TO SEE WHICH OF THE TWO IS IN FORCE ask GET /api/security, or open the settings panel
;; -- which writes this section for you, so the list can be kept up to date without
;; editing this file by hand.
;;
;; `~` MEANS YOUR OPERATING SYSTEM'S HOME, not ~/.clj-harness: these are the host's
;; conventions, and relocating CLJ_HARNESS_HOME does not move your keys out from under
;; the guard -- see harness.cap.providers/default-sensitive-paths.
